WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.778
CompTIA Network Plus, domain 2, Network implementation, which is 20 percent of the exam.

00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:10.339
This lesson covers objective 2 point 3.

00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:15.132
Given a scenario, select and configure wireless devices and technologies.

00:00:15.132 --> 00:00:20.450
Today's concept: what antenna gain really does, and which antenna fits which job.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.000
Antenna gain, measured in dBi, does not create power.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.290
The same energy is just pushed into a different shape.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.723
Raise the gain of an omnidirectional antenna and the round donut flattens.

00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:37.540
You reach farther sideways, but coverage above and below gets weaker.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.830
That is why a high gain omnidirectional antenna is a poor choice for a multi-story building.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.710
For general coverage, a ceiling access point uses an omnidirectional antenna.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:56.710
For a long point to point link between two buildings, use a highly directional antenna, such as a Yagi or a parabolic dish.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:57.885
One more trap.

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:04.700
Turning up transmit power on every access point makes the cells bigger, so they overlap more and compete for airtime.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.037
For the exam, antenna gain in dBi does not add power.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:14.045
It reshapes the same energy into a different pattern.

00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:20.627
Ceiling access points use omnidirectional antennas, and long building-to-building links use highly directional ones.

00:01:20.627 --> 00:01:22.500
That's objective two point three.
